Declarative homed user records — operator-authorable, no image rebuild.
======================================================================

Drop <name>.user here, then enable the matching unit:

    systemctl enable --now myosi-homed-user@<name>.service

/usr/libexec/myosi/homed-user-provision searches this directory FIRST, then
the baked /usr/share/myosi/users/. This directory is on the /etc btrfs subvol
(data-luks), so it is writable at runtime — unlike /usr, which is a read-only
erofs image — and it survives A/B image updates.

The record is the "regular" section of a systemd user record; storage flags
(--storage, --disk-size, --luks-*) are CLI flags applied by the helper, NOT
fields here. See homectl(1) and https://systemd.io/USER_RECORD/.

Example — /etc/myosi/users/alan.user:

    {
        "userName": "alan",
        "uid": 1000,
        "gid": 1000,
        "realName": "Alan Example",
        "homeDirectory": "/home/alan",
        "shell": "/usr/bin/fish",
        "memberOf": ["wheel", "video", "render", "input", "kvm"],
        "preferredLanguage": "en_US.UTF-8",
        "service": "io.systemd.Home",
        "enforcePasswordPolicy": false
    }

The home is created with the bootstrap password `changeme` (override with
MYOSI_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD in a unit drop-in). Rotate it immediately:

    homectl passwd <name>

Notes
-----
- uid 1000 marks the primary user. If that UID already belongs to someone
  else, the helper skips creation and binds groups to the existing owner
  instead — it will not clobber an established user.
- Creation is one-shot, gated on `homectl inspect`. Editing this file later
  does NOT update an existing record; use `homectl update` for that.
- You do not need a file here at all. The documented first-boot path is
  `homectl create` by hand; enabling the unit afterwards still binds sysext
  groups (phase 2 runs whether or not an identity exists).
- Pick the username carefully. systemd 259 has no `homectl rename`.

This README is ignored — only files matching <name>.user are read.
